Subject: [PATCH] daynight: always-start stats worker + persist/record forced mode + derive boot running_mode; configwatcher: clean pipeline restart on encoder config change
Target: prudynt-t @ f4b32289359d176fb0795d9f3735af0bd9d6d2fd (branch stable)

This patch fixes two independent reliability problems on T23 / isp_bypass=true.

==== Part 1: day/night stats + forced mode (Config/DayNightWorker/IMPSystem/main) ====
 (A) AE/day-night telemetry (total_gain, ev, ae_luma, brightness_percent) read
     -1 for the whole process life when daynight.enabled=false, because the
     DayNightWorker -- the sole writer of those values -- was only started when
     enabled. Fix: always start the worker; automatic switching stays gated on
     daynight.enabled inside the loop.
 (B) A forced mode did not survive reboot and the live mode was misreported.
     daynight.force_mode was not a persisted config key; the worker never tagged
     the live mode for a forced camera (reported "unknown"); and the ISP was
     hard-forced to Day at the end of IMPSystem::init(). Fix: register
     daynight.force_mode as a persisted string key; the worker RECORDS it at
     startup (current + live_mode, suppress auto-inference) WITHOUT apply_mode()
     on the live pipeline (apply_mode at startup tears down the cold-boot
     encoder, and a deferred apply causes a visible boot-time flip).
 (C) A forced color/mono mode did not take at boot. On this sensor a
     set_running_mode at the END of init does NOT stick, only the EARLY one does.
     Fix: derive image.running_mode from daynight.force_mode BEFORE the early
     set_running_mode; drop the late re-assert (it was a no-op that also added a
     redundant Night sensor switch which intermittently stalled the cold boot).
 The IR-CUT filter + IR LED at boot are handled by the rootfs boot init
 (S56ircut) reading the same daynight.force_mode -- single source of truth.

==== Part 2: config-file changes apply cleanly, no freeze (ConfigWatcher/Config) ====
 Changing an encoder setting (bitrate, resolution, fps, stream format/mode) by
 editing /etc/prudynt.json froze the stream until a process restart. ConfigWatcher
 called cfg->load() (repopulates members) but never rebuilt the encoder, while
 VideoWorker reads cfg->streamN fields live every frame -> worker/encoder mismatch
 -> permanent freeze. The JSON API path avoids this by setting global_restart_*
 and notifying the main loop, which cleanly tears down + rebuilds the affected
 threads.
 Fix: CFG::reload_and_diff() reparses under a single configMutex hold and diffs
 the encoder-/pipeline-defining fields (per-stream geometry+RC, JPEG, sensor dims,
 OSD-enable / RTP-sink format+resolution / audio reconfig) before vs after, and
 ConfigWatcher fires ONLY the needed global_restart_video/rtsp/audio flag(s) under
 mutex_main + notifies the same proven main-loop restart machinery. Benign edits
 (image ISP tuning, OSD content, daynight incl. force_mode, motion params, rtsp
 network knobs) set no flag -> zero stream disruption. load() is split into a
 lock-held load_locked() core so reload_and_diff() reuses it without recursive
 locking; configMutex and mutex_main are never nested (no lock-order inversion).
 Adversarially reviewed: deadlock-free, no stuck joins (every torn-down worker
 honors its restart flag), no torn-state crash (const char* members are strdup'd,
 never freed on reload), selective (no needless restarts).

Verified to apply cleanly to the pinned commit (git apply -p1 and patch -p1).

